---
Attacked the claim (all three migration.go helpers now publish via
s.atomicWrite, staging under randomised StagingSuffix, rename-publish)
independently of the primary lane's writeup.

What I verified myself, not by trusting the report:

- Re-ran both oracles from clean, myself: `.swarm/tier3/F1/accept.sh` ->
  passed=7 failed=0; `.swarm/tier3/F2/accept.sh` -> passed=6 failed=0.
  `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` clean.
- Deadlock: read atomicWrite's body directly (storage.go:570-599) and
  confirmed by grep that it calls no *Storage method and takes no lock
  (`awk` over the function body matching `\bs\.[A-Za-z]+\(|Lock\(|Unlock\(`
  -> no output). All three callers already hold s.mu exclusively
  (EnableEncryptionWithProvider / DisableEncryption, migration.go:25-26,
  116-117) before calling the helpers, so no new lock is acquired inside
  the critical section. invalidateCache only takes cacheMu (storage.go:243-
  248), consistent with the existing s.mu -> cacheMu ordering elsewhere.
- Staging-leak reachability: read all three helpers' skip/early-return
  paths (already-encrypted skip in encryptFileWithRecipient, not-encrypted
  skip in decryptFileWithIdentity, read-failure/not-encrypted/decrypt-
  failure `continue`s in rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity) and confirmed none
  of them can execute after atomicWrite has created a staging file, because
  atomicWrite is called strictly after every skip check in all three. No
  reachable leak in the shipped code (this matches the primary lane's own
  conclusion that its E1b mutation was hypothetical, not present as-shipped).
- Compared against pristine 1512086: encryptFileWithRecipient and
  decryptFileWithIdentity already staged-and-renamed before this task
  (tmpPath := path + ".tmp"; os.WriteFile; os.Rename) — F2's actual change
  for those two is only the staging-name randomisation. rollbackEncryption-
  WithIdentity is the one helper whose publish mechanism actually changed
  (bare os.WriteFile(path, ...) -> s.atomicWrite(path, ...)).

Two live behavioural side effects I found and verified with scratch tests
(never committed; confirmed clean via `git status --porcelain` after each):

1. Permission reset on rollback. Wrote
   TestAdversarialRollbackPermissionReset: chmod a destination to 0600
   before calling rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity. Result: file ends at
   0644, not 0600. Root cause: os.WriteFile onto an *existing* file (the
   pristine rollback's mechanism) does not change the file's mode, but
   atomicWrite always chmods the staging file to the hardcoded 0644 before
   renaming it over the destination, unconditionally replacing whatever
   mode bits were there. This is a genuine, silent behaviour change from
   rollback's own predecessor: no error, no log line, permissions are just
   quietly loosened. It is not new for encrypt/decrypt (they already had
   this property pre-F2), only for rollback.
2. Same root cause, symlink destination: TestAdversarialSymlinkDestination
   showed rollback via rename replaces a symlink destination with a
   regular file rather than writing through it, breaking the link.

Neither is data loss or a torn file — no content is corrupted, no bytes
are lost, and the destination always ends up either the correct plaintext
or the correct ciphertext. Both are silent metadata/topology changes on a
narrow, arguably-not-reachable-in-this-codebase input (a data file under
baseDir with hand-set restrictive permissions or a symlink in place of a
regular file). They are real and I verified them myself rather than taking
the report's word, but they fall outside every criterion this task was
scoped against (atomicity, read-only-destination publish, legacy-name
removal, no leftovers, F1 properties, no deadlock) and they extend a
tradeoff (chmod-then-rename via the shared atomicWrite) that the rest of
this file and package already rely on for the other two helpers and for
every other writer in storage.go. Recording as a finding for a future task,
not as grounds to fail this one.

Tried and failed to break: read-only-destination publish (independently
corroborated atomicWrite's mechanism -- MkdirAll, CreateTemp in the same
directory, Write, Close, Chmod, os.Rename -- rename needs dir write, not
target write, so the primary lane's read that the test isn't passing for a
spurious reason holds up); F1's cache-invalidation bracket placement
(re-ran F1's own placement-mutation oracle myself, 7/7); no-deadlock
(traced the call graph myself rather than trusting the writeup);
race-safety of the whole package (both oracles include `go test -race`
and both passed under my own run).

VERDICT: PASS.
